WEBVTT

0
00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.070
Welcome.

1
00:00:05.449 --> 00:00:09.270
The era of agentic and physical AI is here,

2
00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:12.149
driving an explosion in compute demand.

3
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.659
Today's AI models are larger and more complex than ever before.

4
00:00:20.059 --> 00:00:24.940
Modern AI models are trained on thousands of GPUs working together.

5
00:00:25.299 --> 00:00:26.299
During training,

6
00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.920
these GPUs continuously exchange data

7
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:31.600
to stay synchronized.

8
00:00:31.819 --> 00:00:33.619
As AI clusters grow,

9
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.779
efficient communication between GPUs becomes just

10
00:00:37.779 --> 00:00:40.360
as important as compute performance.

11
00:00:41.439 --> 00:00:42.740
By the end of the decade,

12
00:00:43.159 --> 00:00:48.720
the industry expects to deploy over 150 million AI accelerators.

13
00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:51.720
But there is a hidden bottleneck

14
00:00:51.939 --> 00:00:53.299
in an AI cluster,

15
00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.319
Network performance dictates overall system performance.

16
00:00:58.849 --> 00:01:02.360
Traditional electrical packet switching networks or

17
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:03.479
EPS

18
00:01:03.779 --> 00:01:07.260
are struggling to maintain the velocity required

19
00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:11.610
by rapidly expanding high density AI environments.

20
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:16.559
They face severe challenges with massive interconnect scale,

21
00:01:17.099 --> 00:01:18.610
extreme bandwidth needs,

22
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.019
and sustainable power consumption.

23
00:01:22.089 --> 00:01:25.709
Every time a signal passes through a traditional switch,

24
00:01:26.089 --> 00:01:28.110
it undergoes an optical,

25
00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:29.010
electrical,

26
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.449
optical conversion.

27
00:01:31.209 --> 00:01:33.680
The data must be converted from light

28
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.120
to electricity,

29
00:01:35.419 --> 00:01:36.339
buffered,

30
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:37.440
routed,

31
00:01:37.739 --> 00:01:39.550
and converted back to light.

32
00:01:41.129 --> 00:01:46.330
This process consumes massive amounts of power and adds latency.

33
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.250
The solution to this bottleneck

34
00:01:49.389 --> 00:01:51.410
is optical circuit switching,

35
00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:51.849
or

36
00:01:51.989 --> 00:01:52.900
OCS.

37
00:01:53.139 --> 00:01:55.730
OCS creates fully transparent

38
00:01:56.309 --> 00:01:59.650
direct photonic connections between endpoints.

39
00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:04.010
It completely eliminates the OEO conversion process

40
00:02:04.309 --> 00:02:07.370
by operating purely in the optical domain.

41
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:12.229
OCS is bufferless and operates with near zero jitter.

42
00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:13.360
Because

43
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.630
it simply redirects light,

44
00:02:15.899 --> 00:02:18.529
OCS is inherently protocol

45
00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:20.610
and data rate agnostic.

46
00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:26.149
It can support future network speeds without requiring a hardware replacement.

47
00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:28.009
Best of all,

48
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.580
it dramatically reduces network power consumption.

49
00:02:31.889 --> 00:02:32.429
In fact,

50
00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.470
an OCS switch

51
00:02:34.649 --> 00:02:37.869
can operate at less than 5% the power

52
00:02:38.009 --> 00:02:40.369
of a traditional electrical switch.

53
00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.059
In this lesson,

54
00:02:43.419 --> 00:02:47.320
we explore the limitations of traditional packet switching

55
00:02:47.619 --> 00:02:50.820
and how optical circuit switching overcomes them.

56
00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:52.839
In the next lesson,

57
00:02:53.199 --> 00:02:56.300
we uncover how optical circuit switching differs

58
00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.479
from traditional electrical packet switching.